{"requests": [
 {"kind": "fits", "text": "Kickoff call with a mid-size logistics client on Thursday. All the brief says is 'leadership wants an AI strategy, budget approved, show us quick wins.' They run dispatch, a customer support desk (about 40 agents, mostly email, some phone), invoicing, and a warehouse team that still does a lot in spreadsheets. I don't know where to start. How do I figure out which of these areas would actually benefit, choose one to pilot, and decide what baseline numbers to ask for so we can prove it worked in 3 months? Also, I half suspect some of this is just a rules engine or better Sheets automation and not an agent at all. How do I tell the difference without coming across like I'm talking them out of the project?"},
 {"kind": "fits", "text": "ok so my manager just told me our insurance client 'wants agents' lol. no use case, no data access yet, nothing. we're a GCP/ADK shop so the assumption is we'll build something on Vertex. before I write a single line of code, can you help me put together a short discovery framework? questions to ask the ops people, how to rank candidate problems (value vs. feasibility vs. risk), and what metrics to pull today (handling time, error rates, whatever) so we have a before/after. If you think claims triage would be the obvious first pick, tell me why or why not."},
 {"kind": "elsewhere", "text": "We already decided with the client: the first project is an ADK agent that triages incoming support tickets and drafts replies, and the success metric is median first-response time (currently 6h 20m). I've got a root agent with two sub-agents in agent.py but routing is flaky. About 30% of billing tickets end up with the general-replies sub-agent. Here are the instructions and descriptions for each sub-agent. Why is the delegation going wrong, and how do I fix it?", "needs_instead": "Debugging an existing ADK multi-agent setup: fixing sub-agent descriptions and instructions so the LLM routes billing tickets correctly. The use case and metrics are already chosen, so no discovery or prioritization work is needed."}
]}
